# Purpose

This rule supplements `latex_writer.mdc` with provisions that are specific to the I\textsuperscript{2}C family of verification papers in this workspace.
`latex_writer.mdc` governs scientific writing, anti-plagiarism, scope discipline, forbidden-phrase sweeps, and reference freshness.
This file governs the additional discipline needed so that multiple I\textsuperscript{2}C manuscripts authored from the same codebase do not collide on symbols, terminology, UVM names, or reference surface, and do not drift away from the house convention on folder structure.

When this rule conflicts with `latex_writer.mdc`, `latex_writer.mdc` wins.
When this rule is silent, defer to `latex_writer.mdc`.

# Symbol registry across I2C sibling papers

Every I\textsuperscript{2}C sibling paper in this workspace consults this registry before binding a symbol at a display equation.
Symbols that are already bound in a sibling manuscript must not be re-bound to a different meaning, even for a different physical quantity.
Reusing a letter is permitted only if it is given a distinguishing subscript, superscript, or accent, AND the equation is accompanied by a one-sentence disambiguation clause that names the sibling and the sibling's original binding.

| Symbol | Paper of origin | Meaning | Notes |
|---|---|---|---|
| `B(t)` | `deterministic_dual_role_i2c_verification/access.tex` | Bus state at time `t` (idle, start, data, stop) | Do not reuse in other siblings for buffer state |
| `D^{(i)}` | `deterministic_dual_role_i2c_verification/access.tex` | Per-agent drive-enable for agent `i` | Distinct from credit paper's `D` (receiver capacity) |
| `d^{(i)}` | `deterministic_dual_role_i2c_verification/access.tex` | Per-agent drive-decision signal for agent `i` | Lowercase variant, distinct from `D^{(i)}` |
| `Role(t)` | `deterministic_dual_role_i2c_verification/access.tex` | Agent role at time `t` (master or slave) | Dual-role scope only |
| `c(t)` | `credit_based_flow_control_verification/access.tex` | Transmitter's live credit counter at time `t` | Scalar, non-negative integer |
| `D` | `credit_based_flow_control_verification/access.tex` | Receiver FIFO capacity (`cbr_depth`) | Bound to depth config knob |
| `f(t)` | `credit_based_flow_control_verification/access.tex` | Credits in flight (consumed but not yet returned) | Conservation: `c(t) + f(t) = D` |
| `cbr_pipe_delay` | `credit_based_flow_control_verification/access.tex` | Return-path pipeline depth in cycles | Config knob, integer |
| `T_stall` | `credit_based_flow_control_verification/access.tex` | CBT stall timeout derived from depth and pipe delay | `cbr_depth * cbr_pipe_delay * t_LOW` |

A paper that introduces a new I\textsuperscript{2}C symbol appends a row here and commits the row together with the manuscript.
A paper that reuses a registry symbol with a subscript adds a disambiguation sentence at the defining equation in the form:
"We adopt `<symbol>` for `<meaning>`; the `<sibling paper slug>` manuscript's `<conflicting symbol>` denoting `<sibling meaning>` is unrelated and not reused here."

# Terminology lock-in

- The protocol name in prose is `I\textsuperscript{2}C`. The forms `I2C`, `IIC`, `i2c`, and `I^2C` must not appear in prose or in captions. Identifiers inside code listings (file names, class names, ports) that contain the ASCII spelling `i2c` are exempt because the tools treat them as source text rather than prose.
- Bus signals in prose are `\texttt{scl}` and `\texttt{sda}`; open-drain contributions are `\texttt{scl\_drive}` and `\texttt{sda\_drive}`. The capitalized forms `SCL` and `SDA` are used only in figure legends, in the I\textsuperscript{2}C specification citation, or when contrasting electrical behavior.
- I\textsuperscript{2}C specification timing parameters are written with the mathrm form and tied to their units with a non-breaking space: `$t_{\mathrm{BUF}}$`, `$t_{\mathrm{LOW}}$`, `$t_{\mathrm{HIGH}}$`, `$t_{\mathrm{SU;STA}}$`, `$t_{\mathrm{HD;DAT}}$`, `$t_{\mathrm{VD;DAT}}$`.
- The bus frequency is written `$100$~kHz` (Standard Mode), `$400$~kHz` (Fast Mode), `$1$~MHz` (Fast Mode Plus), `$3.4$~MHz` (High-Speed Mode), `$5$~MHz` (Ultra Fast-Mode); always with a non-breaking space between numeral and unit, and the mode name is stated on first mention.
- Reference to the I\textsuperscript{2}C specification uses the NXP UM10204 citation (`b1` in the shared reference bank) and the phrase "I\textsuperscript{2}C-Bus Specification and User Manual" verbatim; paraphrased titles such as "I\textsuperscript{2}C standard" or "I\textsuperscript{2}C protocol manual" are avoided in references.
- Enumerate and itemize items do not apply `\textbf{...}` to the leading label. A trailing colon is sufficient to distinguish label from body, and when the label itself is a full clause a trailing period is preferred. Bold inside item labels creates visual noise, doubles the cognitive weight of the list, and is rejected by review; the `\item \textbf{Label:} body` pattern is mechanically rewritten to `\item Label: body` before commit.

# Canonical UVM naming across siblings

The shared testbench is the canonical source. Paper manuscripts that reference the codebase must use the exact names below.

- Environment: `i2c_env`
- Agent: `i2c_agent`
- Driver: `i2c_driver` (base), specializations `i2c_master_driver`, `i2c_slave_driver`, `i2c_credit_master_driver`, `i2c_credit_slave_driver`
- Monitor: `i2c_monitor` (base), specialization `i2c_credit_monitor`
- Sequencer: `i2c_sequencer`
- Scoreboard: `i2c_scoreboard`
- Coverage collector: `i2c_coverage`
- Event pool: `i2c_event_pool`
- Config object: `i2c_config`
- Sequence item: `i2c_seq_item`

Paper-specific configuration knobs use the paper's slug as a prefix to avoid collision in the shared config object: `cfg.cbr_depth`, `cfg.cbr_pipe_delay`, `cfg.credit_mode_enable`, `cfg.dual_role_switch_gate`, `cfg.bus_ownership_invariant_enable`.
A new I\textsuperscript{2}C paper that introduces a new config knob adds the knob to the shared `i2c_config` and documents it in this rule file.

# Protocol-specific hedges

Replace free-standing adjectives such as "deterministic", "regression-friendly", "scalable", or "robust" with bounded-scope hedges that state the envelope in the same clause.
Preferred I\textsuperscript{2}C-specific forms:

- "at the operating point of I\textsuperscript{2}C Standard Mode (`$100$~kHz`) with `$t_{\mathrm{BIT}} = 10~\mu\mathrm{s}$`"
- "within the tested `$t_{\mathrm{BUF}}$` envelope of N to M cycles"
- "on the co-simulated RTL slave with an 8-entry FIFO and `cbr_pipe_delay` of 1--4 cycles"
- "under the evaluated burst-length sweep of `$B \in \{1, 4, 8, 16, 32\}$`"
- "deterministic within the tested `cbr_pipe_delay` envelope; behavior outside the tested envelope is a subject of Section III's limitations"
- "regression-friendly in the sense that same-seed reruns reproduce the shadow-counter trace bit-for-bit across the 90-run campaign"

Unbounded adjectives are rejected by review.

