Suggested routing rules when a paper needs to cite a pre-2024 concept:
- NoC credit-based virtual channels: route through Ma et al. 2025 (NoCFuzzer) rather than through Dally 2001 or Bjerregaard 2006.
- I\textsuperscript{2}C clock-stretching semantics: route through Berik 2026 or Lu 2025 rather than through Salunkhe 2017.
- UVM LRM reference: if a concrete section of the LRM is not cited, omit the entry rather than carrying it as a pre-2024 line item; cite a concrete assertion or class when the LRM is named.
- SystemVerilog reference: similarly, cite a concrete language feature in use; omit the 2005 IEEE 1800 entry if the feature is not explicitly named.

## IEEE-first preference ladder

When choosing a reference for a new technical claim, walk this ladder top-down and pick the first venue that carries the claim. Picking lower on the ladder when a higher-ladder option exists is rejected by review.

1. IEEE journal (Access, TCAD, TVLSI, TCAS-I, TCAS-II, TDSC, Micro, Computer, Design & Test).
2. IEEE-sponsored conference (DAC, DATE, ICCAD, ISCAS, VTS, ITC, ISVLSI, HOST, AsianHOST, ATS, NorCAS, IOLTS).
3. ACM journal or conference (TODAES, TACO, HPCA, MICRO, ISCA).
4. Springer / Elsevier / MDPI peer-reviewed journal (Electronics, Microprocessors and Microsystems, Journal of Supercomputing, Systems).
5. arXiv preprint, or camera-ready pre-publication at a top venue accepted but not yet indexed.

Hard floor for IEEE Access submissions authored from this workspace: at least twenty cited references, of which at least one-third (ceiling of `total / 3`) sit at ladder level 1 or 2. When this floor is not met, the manuscript returns to the research stage rather than padding with lower-ladder citations. A soft target of "at least half at ladder level 1 or 2" applies as a quality indicator for subsequent revisions, but is not a review-blocker on the initial submission.

