## Matplotlib and seaborn legend discipline

Diagrams produced from `img/seaborn/*.py` ship both `.png` (for on-screen review) and `.pdf` (for LaTeX inclusion) via two explicit `plt.savefig` calls with `bbox_inches='tight'`. A diagram that ships only the PNG is rejected by review because the LaTeX build otherwise rasterizes its input.

Auxiliary legends (line-type keys, swatch keys, numbered-annotation boxes) must not overlap each other, any data region, or any annotation text. Concretely:

- Prefer a single-row legend placed along the top or bottom margin of the axes, with at most five horizontal columns. A two-row legend is acceptable only when the first row is line-type keys and the second row is a numbered annotation box placed strictly below the first row with a minimum vertical gap of `0.15` axis units.
- When mixing a swatch legend with a numbered annotation box, either (a) place the swatch in the same top row as the line-type keys to keep the numbered box isolated, or (b) increase the vertical gap between rows until a visual inspection confirms non-overlap; inspection is part of the pre-commit checklist, not a runtime assertion.
- Module-scope constants for legend geometry (`leg_x`, `leg_y_top`, `leg_y_bot`, `leg_box_x`, `leg_box_y`, `leg_box_w`, `leg_box_h`) are declared at the top of the plotting function so that future edits do not require tracing through axis-relative offsets.
- The absolute position of each legend element is computed relative to the module-scope constants rather than to hardcoded literals inside the drawing loop. Hardcoded literals for legend coordinates are rejected because they break overlap review when the figure is resized.
- Caption-line count for diagrams citing numbered callouts (`\ding{172}` through `\ding{181}`) is kept at nine or fewer; a callout count above nine breaks the visual mapping between diagram and caption and is instead split across two subpanels.

Cross-check: every architecture diagram regenerated during a revision pass produces both a PNG and a PDF, and the PNG is opened for visual inspection of legend overlap before the corresponding `.tex` build is requested.

## LaTeX float-placement rules

Float specifiers carry verification-adjacent meaning because aggressive `[!t]` placement in a two-column template collides with `lstlisting` density and produces the whitespace artifacts that a careful reader reads as "the authors did not run the manuscript".

- Single-column figures default to `[!htbp]`, not `[!t]`. A global `\floatplacement{figure}{htbp}` is declared in the preamble together with `\usepackage{float}` so that a forgotten specifier still routes through the default.
- Column-spanning `figure*` uses `[!t]` only, and only when `stfloats` is loaded in the preamble; `[!b]` on a column-spanning float is rejected because the IEEE Access template does not reserve a matching bottom-of-column slot.
- Text-float separation is tightened in the preamble with `\setlength{\textfloatsep}{8pt plus 2pt minus 2pt}`, `\setlength{\intextsep}{6pt plus 2pt minus 2pt}`, and `\setlength{\dbltextfloatsep}{8pt plus 2pt minus 2pt}`. These values are preserved across revision rounds unless the page count explicitly requires looser spacing.
- Listing-density threshold: if a section contains three or more `lstlisting` blocks, it must not also contain more than two `[!t]` figure floats. If the combined budget is exceeded, the single-column figures are demoted to `[!htbp]` and `\floatplacement{figure}{htbp}` is re-declared locally with `\begingroup...\endgroup` around the dense subsection.
- Per-listing length is capped at 25 source lines. Longer listings are split, with the caption clarifying the split (e.g., "Listing 4a / 4b: credit monitor, TBI-1 enforcement / TBI-6 enforcement") so that LaTeX does not orphan the caption from its code block.
- Every `\begin{figure}[!t]` (not `figure*`) introduced during a revision pass is verified by grep against the manuscript before commit; a non-zero hit count converts to `[!htbp]` or promotes the figure to `figure*` depending on whether the content is single- or column-spanning.
- Column-spanning `figure*[!t]` is preferred whenever column-width rendering would make tick labels, annotation text, or legend entries illegible at the final page scale. Architecture block diagrams, wide comparison tables, time-series traces whose horizontal axis has more than ten major ticks, raincloud plots with multiple category columns, and regression bar charts with five or more scenarios therefore default to `figure*[!t]` with `width=0.85\linewidth` to `0.88\linewidth`. Narrow single-shot plots (initialization waterfalls, single-panel histograms) stay at `figure[!htbp]` with `width=0.92\linewidth` to `0.98\linewidth` so they render close to the referencing paragraph. The round-3 whitespace artifact that motivated a single-column default no longer applies because `\raggedbottom` (re-issued after `\maketitle`) and the mandatory minipage wrap for every `lstlisting` remove the vertical-glue stretch and the listing-split mechanisms that produced the gap; readability is the governing criterion.
- Manuscripts containing five or more `lstlisting` blocks add `\raggedbottom`, `\widowpenalty=10000`, and `\clubpenalty=10000` to the preamble directly after the `\dbltextfloatsep` line, to prevent `\flushbottom` from stretching vertical glue around deferred floats and to suppress orphan/club lines around listings.
- The IEEE Access class (`ieeeaccess.cls`) re-issues `\flushbottom` inside its `\maketitle` expansion, so the preamble `\raggedbottom` alone is insufficient. Every manuscript using `ieeeaccess.cls` repeats the three lines (`\raggedbottom`, `\widowpenalty=10000`, `\clubpenalty=10000`) immediately after `\maketitle` so the ragged-bottom policy survives class initialization. A missing post-`\maketitle` `\raggedbottom` is the single most common cause of persistent whitespace around listings after a round-1 fix has already tightened float separations.
- Every `lstlisting` block is wrapped in `\noindent\begin{minipage}{\linewidth} ... \end{minipage}` so that the code body and its caption are atomic and cannot split across column boundaries. Column splits are the second most common cause of whitespace around listings: when an unwrapped `lstlisting` is cut between columns, `captionpos=b` attaches the caption to the fragment carrying the last code line, leaving the other column with stretched glue above the fragment. The minipage wrap eliminates the split entirely; the listing either fits in the remaining column or starts the next column as a single unit. An unwrapped `\begin{lstlisting}` in a manuscript with five or more listings is rejected by review.
- Global caption separation lengths `\abovecaptionskip` and `\belowcaptionskip` are pinned to `4pt` and `2pt` respectively in the preamble, so that the listings package's local `abovecaptionskip=0.5em` is not shadowed by an IEEE Access default that reinstalls a stretchier `10pt plus 2pt minus 2pt` value during class loading.

# Baseline-comparison discipline

Every I\textsuperscript{2}C verification manuscript carries an explicit quantitative trade-off discussion against an alternative that the proposed mechanism replaces or augments. The discussion takes one of two forms, and the form is named honestly in the figure title and prose:

- **Measured comparison.** Both columns of the comparison are measured under the same testbench harness, the same workload, and the same simulator build. The generator script is committed under `img/seaborn/` and the regression CSVs that back each measured column are committed under `sim/` (or wherever the regression target writes them). A measured comparison is the default form whenever both modes can be exercised in the existing testbench by toggling a config knob (e.g. `cfg.<feature>_enable`).
- **Analytical illustration.** When one or both columns cannot be measured at the current revision pass (e.g. the alternative requires a separate testbench, or the comparison quantity is a closed-form bound rather than a regression metric), the figure is presented as a single-curve analytical illustration rather than a two-column comparison. The figure title contains the word "Analytical" verbatim, the y-axis names what the curve represents (e.g. "overhead", not "efficiency", to avoid suggesting a measurement substitution), and the prose states the closed-form expression so a reviewer can re-derive the curve without opening the repository. The generator script is committed under `img/seaborn/` with the naming pattern `generate_<topic>_overhead.py` or `generate_<topic>_bound.py`.

A "two-column comparison" in which both columns are modeled (no measurement is substituted in either column) is rejected by review because it gives the reader no way to judge the asymmetry: it should be reformulated either as a single-curve analytical illustration or as a measured comparison once the alternative is exercisable. The previous "(anchored)" / "(modeled)" column-header convention is retired because it proved to be misleading in practice when neither column was anchored to a fresh measurement.

The Discussion section's "Comparison with ..." subsection cites the trade-off figure by `\ref` at least once and reports the relationship either as a Pareto (one form wins on metric A, the other wins on metric B), as a crossover (one form wins until parameter X, the other wins thereafter), or as a single-quantity bound (the proposed mechanism pays a closed-form cost in exchange for a named guarantee). The framing is chosen to match the actual data; an honest single-quantity bound is preferred over a forced two-column comparison when only one column is exercisable.

Generators backing the trade-off discussion are written for Python 3.5+ (no f-strings) so they run on the workspace's `/router/bin/python3` interpreter without requiring a virtualenv; this matches the convention of the other `img/seaborn/generate_*.py` generators in this workspace.

# Code and data availability discipline

Every IEEE Access submission authored from this workspace carries a `\section*{Code and Data Availability}` subsection at the bottom of the manuscript, immediately before the References. The subsection cites the public repository URL `https://github.com/tobliao/i2c_uvm` (rendered with `\url{...}`) and names the simulator and Python toolchain versions used to produce the regression results.

The subsection contains a paper-to-repo artifact map at listing, figure, and table granularity: every numbered listing maps to one source file in `src/`; every numbered figure maps to one generator under `img/seaborn/`; every numbered table maps to one regression target in `sim/Makefile` (or to one generator script if the table is composed of analytical numbers). The map uses `\ref{}` against the manuscript's own labels so that a reader scanning the artifact map sees the same numbering as in the body text. Stale paths are caught by the verification step in the pre-commit checklist below.

A companion `README.md` at the repository root mirrors the same artifact map, so a reader who lands on the repository (e.g. via the GitHub URL in the manuscript) can locate the regression target, the figure generator, and the source file for every numbered manuscript artifact without opening the PDF. The README also carries the BibTeX citation snippet for every published or submitted manuscript that this repository underwrites; placeholder entries are marked `note = {to appear}` or `note = {submitted}` until the venue confirms.

When the manuscript names a regression target by string (e.g. `make regr_credit`), the string is verified against the actual `sim/Makefile` before commit; a string that does not resolve to an actual target is treated as a reproducibility defect rather than a typographic one.

# Folder-naming convention for I2C papers

The `paper/` directory contains one subdirectory per manuscript.
Subdirectory slugs are lowercase, underscore-separated, no spaces, no leading articles except the reserved `a_` prefix used for methodology-focused papers whose title genuinely opens with an indefinite article.

- Verification-focused papers use the pattern `<descriptor>_<mechanism>_verification`.
  Examples: `credit_based_flow_control_verification`, `deterministic_dual_role_i2c_verification`, `clock_stretching_bounded_timeout_verification`.
- Methodology-focused papers use the pattern `a_<noun_phrase>`.
  Examples: `a_context_aware_feedback_loop`.

Each folder contains a single top-level `.tex` file:
- `access.tex` for IEEE Access submissions.
- `main.tex` for MDPI journals (e.g., Electronics).

Sibling image directories are `img/seaborn/` for plotted figures and `img/bio/` for author biographies.
Image paths in the `.tex` file are relative to the folder (e.g., `img/seaborn/credit_architecture_diagram.png`) so that the folder is self-contained and can be moved without breaking references.
Folders must not contain stray spaces, uppercase letters, or hyphens.
Renames are done with plain `mv` because `paper/` is in `.gitignore`; `git mv` is not required.

# Ten-item anti-plagiarism and reproducibility pre-commit checklist for I2C siblings

Before committing any I\textsuperscript{2}C manuscript, the author runs this check.
The intent is to catch overlaps with existing siblings, not to replace the Turnitin pipeline that `latex_writer.mdc` requires before submission.

1. For each paragraph in the manuscript, grep the first sentence against all other `paper/*/access.tex` and `paper/*/main.tex` files.
   Any paragraph whose first sentence produces a match in another sibling is rewritten at argument-level, not at synonym level.
2. For any sentence shared between the manuscript and a sibling, verify that the manuscript does not share ten or more consecutive substantive words with the sibling.
   Function words (articles, prepositions, pronouns, auxiliaries) are excluded from the count; code identifiers, citation keys, and equation labels are excluded.
3. Verify that a `\paragraph{Paper scope.}` block exists at the end of the Introduction.
   The block contains exactly two sentences: the first is composed of negatives and lists what the manuscript does not claim; the second points forward to a Limitations subsection.
4. Verify that every bound symbol in the manuscript is consistent with the sibling registry in this rule file.
   Any reused letter carries a disambiguation sentence.
5. Run the forbidden-word sweep from `latex_writer.mdc` (forbidden words include `enables`, `crucial`, `novel`, `seamless`, `robust`, `leverage`, `significantly`, `state-of-the-art`, `cutting-edge`).
   Each remaining occurrence is replaced with a concrete verb and accompanied by an operational definition in the same sentence.
6. Count bibliography entries by year.
   At most three entries may predate 2024.
   At least 70% of entries must be dated 2024 or later.
   For IEEE Access submissions: at least twenty entries total, with at least one-third at ladder level 1 or 2 from the IEEE-first preference ladder in this rule file (soft target: half).
   Every entry must be cited at least once; uncited entries are removed.
   Grep the manuscript for the words `Analytical` (in figure titles), `overhead` or `bound` (in y-axis labels) within the trade-off subsection. The trade-off discussion either takes the analytical-illustration form (single-curve, "Analytical" in the title) or the measured-comparison form (both columns regenerated from committed CSVs); a two-column comparison in which both columns are modeled returns to the evaluation stage per the Baseline-comparison discipline section above.
7. Verify tied spaces on every I\textsuperscript{2}C-specific unit and cross-reference: `Figure~\ref`, `Section~\ref`, `Table~\ref`, `Eq.~\eqref`, `$100$~kHz`, `$10~\mu\mathrm{s}$`, `$t_{\mathrm{LOW}}$`, `$t_{\mathrm{BUF}}$`.
   A broken tie is a Turnitin-adjacent correctness defect, not a typographic nicety.
8. Regenerate every architecture diagram and raincloud plot from its `img/seaborn/*.py` source, confirm that both `.png` and `.pdf` are written via `plt.savefig(..., bbox_inches='tight')`, and open each PNG to visually inspect that no legend element overlaps a numbered annotation box, a data region, or another legend column.
   A diagram whose legend geometry changed during a revision pass is re-inspected even if no caption text changed.
9. Grep `\\begin\{figure\}\[!t\]` (single-column) on every manuscript `.tex` file.
   Each hit is converted to `[!htbp]` or promoted to `figure*` depending on whether the content is single- or column-spanning.
   Grep `\\begin\{figure\*\}\[!t\]` as a sanity check that column-spanning floats retain `[!t]` and that `stfloats` is loaded in the preamble.
   Grep `\\item \\textbf\{` on every manuscript `.tex` file; expect zero hits. Each hit is mechanically rewritten to drop the `\textbf{...}` wrapper while preserving the label text and its trailing punctuation, per the no-bold-on-item-labels rule in the Terminology lock-in section above.
   Count `\\begin\{lstlisting\}` and count `\\noindent\\begin\{minipage\}` that immediately precedes an `lstlisting`; the two counts are equal. An unwrapped lstlisting in a manuscript with five or more listings is rejected by review and is mechanically wrapped before the visual-inspection step.
   Grep `\\raggedbottom` on every manuscript `.tex` file; for `ieeeaccess.cls` submissions the count is exactly two---one in the preamble, one immediately after `\maketitle`.
10. Grep `github.com/tobliao/i2c_uvm` and `Code and Data Availability` on every manuscript `.tex` file; expect at least one hit each, per the Code and data availability discipline section above. Verify that the same URL appears in the repository-root `README.md`, that the README's paper artifact map names every numbered listing/figure/table that the manuscript's artifact map names, and that every regression target named in the manuscript prose (e.g. `make regr_credit`) resolves to an actual target in `sim/Makefile`. A missing GitHub URL, a stale regression target name, or a divergence between the manuscript's artifact map and the README's artifact map is a reproducibility defect that returns to commit-prep.

# Future topics parking lot

The following I\textsuperscript{2}C-related topics are reserved for future manuscripts.
Each entry carries a canonical folder slug and a one-line scope disclaimer so that a future paper, possibly authored by a different agent session, starts from a consistent frame rather than drifting.

- `clock_stretching_bounded_timeout_verification`: bound the duration of I\textsuperscript{2}C slave clock stretching as a verification contract, with a falsifiable timeout per stretch and regression-time classification of stretch events. Out of scope: multi-master arbitration, credit accounting.
- `multi_master_arbitration_coherence`: verify arbitration winner selection and bus-ownership hand-off across multiple concurrent masters. Out of scope: slave-side FIFO overflow, dual-role switching of a single agent.
- `i3c_dynamic_address_assignment_uvm`: UVM verification of I\textsuperscript{3}C dynamic address assignment (DAA) procedure including ENTDAA, SETDASA, and RSTDAA flows. Out of scope: legacy I\textsuperscript{2}C-only features, HDR-DDR modes beyond SDR.
- `smbus_pec_integrity_verification`: SMBus Packet Error Code (PEC) integrity verification with CRC-8 generation and detection across block writes and reads. Out of scope: non-SMBus I\textsuperscript{2}C endpoints, ARA (Alert Response Address) flows.
- `repeated_start_ownership_handover`: verify that repeated-start does not break bus ownership invariants when combined with role switching. Out of scope: credit accounting, SMBus-specific framing.
- `open_drain_contention_formal`: formal proof that `scl_drive`/`sda_drive` contributions from multiple agents resolve correctly under open-drain wired-AND semantics. Out of scope: dynamic address assignment, credit accounting.
- `i2c_security_ara_alert_verification`: UVM verification of the SMBus Alert Response Address (ARA) flow and host-alert prioritization. Out of scope: encrypted payloads, TLS-style protocol layers.
- `i2c_target_only_endpoint_conformance`: conformance verification for target-only I\textsuperscript{2}C endpoints that never operate as master, including spurious-master-address response behavior. Out of scope: dual-role switching, credit accounting.

New entries are appended below this paragraph rather than inserted mid-list, so that the parking lot preserves chronological ordering.

# How to use this rule

A new I\textsuperscript{2}C paper begins by:

1. Choosing a slug from the parking lot above, or proposing a new slug that matches the folder-naming convention. Create the folder under `paper/` with the chosen slug.
2. Copying the closest-topic sibling's `access.tex` or `main.tex` as a skeletal starting point, then immediately executing step 1 of the anti-plagiarism checklist against that sibling to prevent inheritance of sentences.
3. Consulting the symbol registry before any display equation; claiming new rows in this rule file when introducing new symbols.
4. Writing the manuscript with protocol-specific hedges; rejecting any unbounded adjective.
5. Running the full ten-item anti-plagiarism and reproducibility pre-commit checklist before the first author review.
6. Updating this rule file with the new paper's contributed symbols, config knobs, and any newly curated references.
